From 71096b8000ef6e8935f406a44a0a3ee219ce5b4c Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: alpha-liu-01 <44378856-alpha-liu-01@users.noreply.gitlab.com>
Date: Thu, 12 Nov 2026 12:32:14 -0500
Subject: [PATCH] server: don't block on the previous frame's present fence

RealHwc2Wrapper::set() waits, after presenting frame N, for the present
fence of frame N-1 (marked "FIXME: is needed at all?"). With Qualcomm's
SDM composer on a 120 Hz panel (Xiaomi Redmi Note 12 5G, SM4375,
Halium 14) that fence signals a vsync late, so the wait costs a whole
extra vsync per frame and the system compositor never exceeds 60 fps.

Back pressure does not depend on this wait: since the HWC2 present-fence
fix, each framebuffer is guarded by the present fence of the frame after
it (update_usage()), so a buffer is not rendered into before the panel
has moved on from it.

Measured on that device, scrolling Lomiri's app drawer with the panel at
120 Hz (frames committed to the panel per second, from the SDE driver's
measured_fps): 55-62 with the wait, 102-105 without it.
---
 src/platforms/android/server/real_hwc2_wrapper.cpp | 8 +++++---
 1 file changed, 5 insertions(+), 3 deletions(-)

diff --git a/src/platforms/android/server/real_hwc2_wrapper.cpp b/src/platforms/android/server/real_hwc2_wrapper.cpp
index 139800e..dfcb0f5 100644
--- a/src/platforms/android/server/real_hwc2_wrapper.cpp
+++ b/src/platforms/android/server/real_hwc2_wrapper.cpp
@@ -319,11 +319,13 @@ void mga::RealHwc2Wrapper::set(
             // android11-gsi/graphics/composer/2.1/utils/hwc2on1adapter/include/hwc2on1adapter/HWC2On1Adapter.h#137
             // and https://source.android.com/docs/core/graphics/sync#hardware_composer_integration
 
-            // Wait for the previously submitted frame to be displayed to ensure we're not rendering too fast
-            // FIXME: is needed at all?
+            // Do not wait here for the previously submitted frame to be
+            // displayed. With some composers (Qualcomm SDM) that wait costs a
+            // whole extra vsync per frame: 60 Hz on a 120 Hz panel. Back
+            // pressure already comes from the framebuffers, each guarded by
+            // the next frame's present fence (update_usage() below).
             auto _last_present_fence = last_present_fence[display_id];
             if (_last_present_fence != -1) {
-                sync_wait(_last_present_fence, -1);
                 close(_last_present_fence);
                 last_present_fence[display_id] = -1;
             }
